From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Niklas=20Hamb=C3=BCchen?= <mail@nh2.me>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 21:21:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mon/OSDMonitor: Prevent late reporters from resetting OSD
 failure start
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Fixes that OSDs of a dead host could be marked down minutes, instead of
seconds, after their heartbeat grace had passed (unnecessarily slow
failover).

Done by measuring an OSD's failure duration from the oldest reports of
the agreeing CRUSH subtrees, instead of from the newest report, as newer
reports restart the failure clock every time heartbeat peers change;
we think that made no sense, because a newer report is no evidence that
the OSD failed later, only that its reporter started watching it later.

Failure scenario:

  When a whole host dies (e.g. it loses power), I/O to PGs whose primary is
  one of its OSDs hangs until the monitors mark that OSD down.

  In our production cluster (v20.2.1, 212 OSDs on 18 hosts), the last OSD of
  a dead host with 12 OSDs was marked down with a large delay (7 min 51 s)
  after the host lost power, with the monitor logging

      after 67.770186 >= grace 62.788019

  The slow failover causes unnecessarily long hangs:
  For example, for us it blocked all of CephFS for those minutes, as the
  replacement MDS waited in `up:rejoin` for metadata objects in such PGs.

Background: How the monitor detects a dead OSD:

  If only an OSD process dies, the kernel of its still-running host refuses
  connections to it, and peers report that to the monitor as an immediate
  failure (`osd_fast_fail_on_connection_refused`), which marks the OSD down
  right away.
  If instead the whole host dies, nothing answers at all (no connection
  refusals or resets), so Ceph has implemented that the OSD is detected
  as down by heartbeat timeouts:
  Every OSD pings a set of heartbeat peers,
  and reports a peer that has not answered for `osd_heartbeat_grace` to the
  monitor, including since when it has not answered (`failed_since`).
  The monitor collects these reports per target OSD in `failure_info_t`.
  So that a single OSD with a bad network link cannot get healthy OSDs
  marked down, the monitor marks the target down only if:

  * reports come from at least `mon_osd_min_down_reporters` different CRUSH
    subtrees (`mon_osd_reporter_subtree_level`, default `host`), and
  * the failure has lasted at least the grace period: `osd_heartbeat_grace`,
    increased by `mon_osd_adjust_heartbeat_grace` for OSDs that have a
    history of being marked down while still alive, to avoid flapping.

  A reporter withdraws its report when it stops having the target as a
  heartbeat peer.

Problem:

  Until now, `OSDMonitor::check_failure()` measured the failure duration as
  `now - failure_info_t::get_failed_since()`, where `get_failed_since()`
  returned the most recent `failed_since` of all current reporters.
  So each reporter that started reporting later moved the start of the
  failure forward. When a host died, this happened systematically:

  * Each OSD marked down creates a new osdmap, after which OSDs re-pick their
    heartbeat peers (`OSD::maybe_update_heartbeat_peers()`).
  * A newly assigned peer of a dead, not yet marked down OSD never gets a
    reply, and reports it with `failed_since` = the time of its own first
    ping (`first_tx` in `OSD::heartbeat_check()`), i.e. a recent time.
  * Peers that stop watching the dead OSD withdraw their older reports
    (`send_still_alive()`, `cancel_report()`), which made the monitor
    recompute the maximum from the remaining, newer reports.

  The OSDs of a dead host usually have different graces (through
  `mon_osd_adjust_heartbeat_grace`), so they are marked down one after
  another, and the remaining ones keep getting fresh reporters:
  Until now, their failure clocks thus kept restarting,
  and more reports made the decision later.
  In the production incident above, the last OSD collected reports from 200
  different OSDs over those minutes, 58 of them already in the first minute.

  Taking the most recent `failed_since` was introduced in commit

      7952c35926c "mon: locally apply osd heartbeat grace to failure checks"

  whose message says:

      Aggregate the failure reports into a single mon 'failed_since' value (the
      max, currently), and wait until we have exceeded the grace period to
      consider the osd failed.

  It gives no reason for the maximum, and "currently" suggests that it was
  not meant as the final choice.
  In any case, it isn't clear to me why that would be a good choice.

Fix:

  With this commit, `check_failure()` takes for each reporting subtree the
  oldest `failed_since` among its reporters, and considers the OSD failed
  since the `mon_osd_min_down_reporters`-th oldest of these, i.e. since the
  time at which that many different subtrees had all observed the failure.
  The requirement that `mon_osd_min_down_reporters` different subtrees agree
  is unchanged, and so is the grace; but a reporter that started watching
  late can no longer hide that other subtrees have observed the failure for
  much longer.

Results:

  Local reproduction:
  3 mons, 18 CRUSH hosts x 10 memstore OSDs, a size-3 pool with 2048 PGs.
  We first give the victim host's OSDs differing laggy histories, as real
  reboots do: Twice, we stop them until they are marked down, and continue
  them one after another, so that each stays down for a different time.
  Through `mon_osd_adjust_heartbeat_grace`, they thus get different graces,
  so that they are later marked down one after another, which causes the
  heartbeat peer re-picks and fresh reports described above.
  Then its 10 OSDs and the leader mon are stopped with SIGSTOP
  (no connections get closed, as with a powered-off host).
  Time from stopping until the 10 OSDs were marked down:

  | version       | runs | last of the 10 OSDs marked down after (s)          | worst delay beyond the OSD's own grace (s) |
  |---------------|------|----------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
  | v20.2.1       | 8    | 37.7, 42.3, 45.4, 49.7, 52.2, 146.0, 192.1, 194.6  | 159                                        |
  | v20.2.1 + fix | 7    | 33.0, 33.2, 33.3, 33.8, 35.9, 36.0, 36.4           | 1.7                                        |

Fixes: https://tracker.ceph.com/issues/83176
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 in Zoo Code, human review
Signed-off-by: Niklas Hambüchen <mail@nh2.me>
---
 src/mon/OSDMonitor.cc | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mon/OSDMonitor.cc b/src/mon/OSDMonitor.cc
index 1a96c741194..3568d527fc0 100644
--- a/src/mon/OSDMonitor.cc
+++ b/src/mon/OSDMonitor.cc
@@ -3375,7 +3375,12 @@ bool OSDMonitor::check_failure(utime_t now, int target_osd, failure_info_t& fi)
     return true;
   }
 
-  set<string> reporters_by_subtree;
+  // For each reporting subtree (e.g. host), the oldest `failed_since` among
+  // its reporters, i.e. since when that subtree has observed the failure.
+  // A reporter that was assigned to `target_osd` as heartbeat peer only
+  // recently reports a recent `failed_since`; that must not hide that other
+  // subtrees have observed the failure for much longer.
+  map<string, utime_t> oldest_failed_since_by_subtree;
   auto reporter_subtree_level = g_conf().get_val<string>("mon_osd_reporter_subtree_level");
   ceph_assert(fi.reporters.size());
   for (auto p = fi.reporters.begin(); p != fi.reporters.end();) {
@@ -3383,11 +3388,17 @@ bool OSDMonitor::check_failure(utime_t now, int target_osd, failure_info_t& fi)
     // fall back to OSD if the level doesn't exist.
     if (osdmap.exists(p->first)) {
       auto reporter_loc = osdmap.crush->get_full_location(p->first);
+      string subtree;
       if (auto iter = reporter_loc.find(reporter_subtree_level);
           iter == reporter_loc.end()) {
-        reporters_by_subtree.insert("osd." + to_string(p->first));
+        subtree = "osd." + to_string(p->first);
       } else {
-        reporters_by_subtree.insert(iter->second);
+        subtree = iter->second;
+      }
+      const utime_t reporter_failed_since = p->second.failed_since;
+      auto [it, inserted] = oldest_failed_since_by_subtree.emplace(subtree, reporter_failed_since);
+      if (!inserted && reporter_failed_since < it->second) {
+        it->second = reporter_failed_since;
       }
       ++p;
     } else {
@@ -3395,10 +3406,21 @@ bool OSDMonitor::check_failure(utime_t now, int target_osd, failure_info_t& fi)
       p = fi.reporters.erase(p);
     }
   }
-  if (reporters_by_subtree.size() < g_conf().get_val<uint64_t>("mon_osd_min_down_reporters")) {
+  const uint64_t min_down_reporters = std::max<uint64_t>(1, g_conf().get_val<uint64_t>("mon_osd_min_down_reporters"));
+  if (oldest_failed_since_by_subtree.size() < min_down_reporters) {
     return false;
   }
-  const utime_t failed_for = now - fi.get_failed_since();
+  // The OSD is considered failed since the time at which `min_down_reporters`
+  // different subtrees had all observed the failure:
+  // The `min_down_reporters`-th oldest of the per-subtree times.
+  vector<utime_t> subtree_failed_since;
+  subtree_failed_since.reserve(oldest_failed_since_by_subtree.size());
+  for (const auto& [subtree, since] : oldest_failed_since_by_subtree) {
+    subtree_failed_since.push_back(since);
+  }
+  const vector<utime_t>::iterator kth_oldest = subtree_failed_since.begin() + (min_down_reporters - 1);
+  std::nth_element(subtree_failed_since.begin(), kth_oldest, subtree_failed_since.end());
+  const utime_t failed_for = now - *kth_oldest;
   const utime_t grace = get_grace_time(now, target_osd, fi);
   if (failed_for >= grace) {
     dout(1) << " we have enough reporters to mark osd." << target_osd
@@ -3409,7 +3431,7 @@ bool OSDMonitor::check_failure(utime_t now, int target_osd, failure_info_t& fi)
 		      << osdmap.crush->get_full_location_ordered_string(
 			target_osd)
 		      << ") ("
-		      << (int)reporters_by_subtree.size()
+		      << (int)oldest_failed_since_by_subtree.size()
 		      << " reporters from different "
 		      << reporter_subtree_level << " after "
 		      << failed_for << " >= grace " << grace << ")";
-- 
